      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n  The `GaugeManager._updateForAfterDistribution()` function uses current voting weights instead of epoch-snapshot weights when calculating reward distributions. This creates a serious timing vulnerability between `update_period()` and `distribute()` calls.\n\n```solidity\nfunction _updateForAfterDistribution(address _gauge) private {\n    address _pool = poolForGauge[_gauge];\n    //uint256 _supplied = weightsPerEpoch[_time][_pool];  //  Commented out\n    uint256 _supplied = IVoter(voter).weights(_pool);    //  Uses current weights\n    \n    if (_supplied > 0) {\n        uint256 _supplyIndex = supplyIndex[_gauge];\n        uint256 _index = index;\n        supplyIndex[_gauge] = _index;\n        uint256 _delta = _index - _supplyIndex;\n        if (_delta > 0) {\n            uint256 _share = _supplied * _delta / 1e18;\n            if (isAlive[_gauge]) {\n                claimable[_gauge] += _share;  //  Never executed if weight = 0\n            }\n        }\n    } else {\n        supplyIndex[_gauge] = index;  //  Index update without rewards\n    }\n}\n```\n\n based commented-out line (`weightsPerEpoch[_time][_pool]`) tells me the development team was aware of the need for epoch-based weight tracking, but this functionality was never implemented. The codebase lacks any `weightsPerEpoch` mapping or snapshot mechanism.\n\nTo be more specific consider the following attack permissionless sequence scenario:\n\nEpoch N, Gauge A has significant voting weight (e.g., 50,000 weight)\nEpoch N+1 begins, Attacker (or any user) calls `Minter.update_period()`\n    This function is publicly callable with no access control \n    Increases `GaugeManager.index` based on current `totalWeight`\n    Transfers emission tokens to `GaugeManager`\nBefore `distribute()` is called Voters call `VoterV3.reset()` to remove votes\n    `reset()` has `onlyNewEpoch` modifier, allowing calls in new epochs (line 122)\n    Reduces gauge weight to 0 or n